 and good solution. And so today we're going to talk about some things in that same vein. And here with us today, we have Sanjay Sadante, Director of Engineering at Acasa. Welcome to the show, Sanjay. Thank you. Thanks for having me. Awesome. Well, we always love to start by learning a bit about how people got to where they are. I think life story is just kind of that interesting thing. Maybe it's because I've grown a little more|||当人们不这样做时，就会遇到这一系列问题。而且这并非唯一的解决方案，但它是一个反复出现且不错的方案。所以今天我们要探讨一些与此同脉相承的话题。今天来到我们节目的嘉宾是 Acasa 的工程总监 Sanjay Sadante。欢迎来到节目，Sanjay。谢谢。谢谢邀请我。太好了。嗯，我们总是喜欢从了解大家是如何走到今天这一步开始。我觉得人生故事就是那么有趣。也许是因为我变得更|||mature, a little older. I like hearing how people also have their own journey and also to just sort of learn the diverse ways people end up in jobs is always just fascinating to me because when people always give career advice, there's always like this one way you do things and it's the way they did things. And so I like hearing that breadth. And so do you have, some people do, some people don't. Do you remember kind of like the first time you either like did programming or your first piece of tech or what got you really excited about this field? Yeah, absolutely. I think the first time I programmed a computer was in eighth grade, and I built a simple website just using HTML and CSS. I didn't even use JavaScript at the time,|||成熟了一点，年纪大了一些。我喜欢听听别人也有他们自己的心路历程，而且了解人们最终从事各种工作的不同途径总是让我觉得非常着迷，因为当人们总是给出职业建议时，总是有一种“你必须这样做事，而这就是他们以前的做法”。所以我喜欢听听这种广泛的经历。那么你——有些人有，有些人没有——你还记得第一次编程，或者接触第一件科技产品，或者是什么让你对这个领域真正感到兴奋的时刻吗？是的，绝对的。我想我第一次给计算机编程是在八年级，我只用了 HTML 和 CSS 构建了一个简单的网站。当时我甚至没有使用 JavaScript，|||and I really liked it. And so all through high school, I ended up building a lot of websites and taking AP computer science. So learned a bit of Java. And I came into college with, I'd say, a fair amount of programming experience and pretty sure that I wanted to major in computer science. Awesome. I also took AP computer science and also did Java. But I'm not curious if they still, do you know you know they still teach it in Java? I'm curious if they've gone to something else. You know, I went back to visit the AP Computer Science class at my first, I guess, academic exposures, but also had been programming a bit before. And so yeah, it's interesting, the web design and sort of so HTML, CSS, being able to, you wanted to do computer science, it sounds like. And then when you went to college, you continued to pursue it? Or did you try something else at first? I had a lot of interests going into college. So I was always taking computer science courses, but also a lot of math classes and also a lot of biology. And I wasn't totally sure how I wanted to mix all of these interests. I had, you know, one idea that I might go to medical school and, you know, be a computational person in that space.|||我真的很喜欢它。所以在整个高中期间，我最终构建了许多网站，并修读了 AP 计算机科学。因此学了一点 Java。我带着可以说相当丰富的编程经验进入大学，并且非常确定自己想主修计算机科学。太棒了。我也修过 AP 计算机科学，也学过 Java。不过我很好奇他们现在是否还在——你知道他们现在还在用 Java 教吗？我很想知道他们是否已经换成别的了。你知道的，我回去拜访了 AP 计算机科学课堂，那是我最初的学术接触，不过我之前也已经做过一些编程了。所以，是的，这很有趣，网页设计以及诸如 HTML、CSS 之类的，能够让你——听起来你想从事计算机科学。然后当你上大学时，你继续深造了吗？还是你一开始尝试了别的？我上大学时有很多兴趣。所以我一直在上计算机科学课程，但也有很多数学课以及很多生物学课。我当时并不是很确定该如何将这些兴趣融合在一起。我有一个想法，就是我可能会去医学院，成为该领域的计算生物学人员，|||And another idea that I might be a software engineer. So it took me a couple years in college to sort of figure out what I was interested in. And I would say, once I heard about bioinformatics for the first time, that's when I realized what I really wanted to do. Both my parents are scientists. And so growing up, I was always really interested in science. And we would talk about biology and chemistry at the dinner table. But I didn't ever realize that there were opportunities for computational folks to work on analyzing big data sets in that space. But once I found that in college, that's what I ended up focusing on for the rest of undergrad and for grad school.|||另一个想法是我可能会成为一名软件工程师。所以在大学的前几年里，我花了一些时间才弄清楚自己真正感兴趣的是什么。我想说的是，当我第一次听到“生物信息学”这个词时，我才意识到这正是我真正想做的事。我的父母都是科学家。所以在成长过程中，我对科学一直非常感兴趣。我们经常在餐桌上讨论生物学和化学。但我从未意识到，计算领域的人才居然有机会在该领域从事大数据集的分析工作。但一旦我在大学里发现了这一点，这就成了我在整个本科剩余时间和研究生阶段专注的方向。|||Oh, nice. So I know the term bioinformatics, and I guess using the prefix and suffix and your context, I could guess. And part of me thinks like things like gene folding, I guess, has been like popular recently in the zeitgeist of, you know, some of the machine learning techniques as applied to those. Is that like the bulk of it is stuff related to that? Or is it I assume it's much broader than that, bioinformatics. Can you maybe tell us a little bit about what did that field? Yeah, absolutely. I think if I had to think of a generic definition, it would be using computational techniques to analyze large data sets in biology. And I can give some examples. So, you know, protein folding is|||哦，不错。我听说过生物信息学（bioinformatics）这个词，我想通过前缀、后缀以及你的语境，我可以猜出来。我有一部分想法是，比如基因折叠之类的，我猜最近在时代精神中很流行，你知道的，一些机器学习技术被应用到了这些方面。这占了其中的大部分内容吗？还是说——我猜生物信息学的范围要广泛得多。你能跟我们讲讲这个领域具体是做什么的吗？当然可以。我想如果要我想一个通用的定义，那就是利用计算技术来分析生物学中的大型数据集。我可以举一些例子。所以，你知道，蛋白质折叠就是|||certainly one example. But the area that I focused on and that I actually worked on in industry for my first couple of companies was analyzing DNA sequencing data coming off of a sequencer. Basically, the idea is, you know, you sequence someone's DNA and you get a bunch of letters out of it, you know, A's, T's, C's, and G's. But how do you figure out what those letters actually mean? How do you figure out where a gene is and if this person has a mutation in that gene? And then once you figure out if there's a mutation, how do you devise computational methods to figure out if that mutation actually causes any effect to the person's health? For example, does that DNA mutation actually lead to a different protein being produced? And will that|||其中一个例子。但我关注的领域，也是我在前几家公司实际从事的工业界领域，是分析从测序仪中输出的 DNA 测序数据。基本上，这个想法是，你知道的，你对某人的 DNA 进行测序，然后从中得到一堆字母，你知道的，A、T、C 和 G。但是你如何弄清楚这些字母实际代表什么意思呢？你如何找出基因在哪里，以及这个人该基因是否有突变？然后，一旦你弄清楚是否存在突变，你如何设计计算方法来弄清楚该突变是否确实对这个人的健康产生了影响？例如，这种 DNA 突变是否真的会导致产生不同的蛋白质？这会|||be harmful to the person? So these are some of the things that I worked on for a few years and I would say are part of bioinformatics as well, is basically, you know, we're getting so much data off of DNA sequencers now that you can no longer analyze it by hand. And so you have to create computational methods to figure out how to make sense of this data at scale. That's awesome. I have this, like, I guess I'll share it publicly, even though it's still fresh in my mind, but always best to do it on a podcast where everyone can listen to it. I think like this, so this thing you're talking about DNA sequencing, I recently fell into some YouTube sort of raffle about DNA synthesis and how actually like it's accessible even for people at you know, at home, I guess, but like semi sophisticated. And so it fell in this whole rabbit hole of like plasmid engineering and like inserting your own sort of DNA synthesis that you designed into like E. coli or into yeast, and then sort of like having the expression of certain proteins,|||对这个人有害吗？所以这些是我工作了几年的其中一些事情，我想这也是生物信息学的一部分，基本上就是，你知道的，我们现在从 DNA 测序仪中获得的数据太多了，你再也无法靠人工来进行分析了。因此，你必须创建计算方法来弄清楚如何在大规模下理解这些数据。太棒了。我有这样一种——我想我会公开分享它，尽管它仍然在我脑海中挥之不去，但在每个人都可以听得到的播客上分享总是最好的。我觉得像这样，你刚才谈到的关于 DNA 测序的事情，我最近陷入了 YouTube 上关于 DNA 合成的某种抽奖活动中，以及实际上它是如何甚至可以让（你知道的）在家里的普通人接触到的，不过是半复杂的。于是我陷入了这样一个深不见底的技术黑洞，比如质粒工程，比如将你设计好的 DNA 合成片段插入到大肠杆菌或酵母中，然后某种程度上让某些蛋白质表达出来，|||either glow in the dark proteins, or even things that are components of milk or whatever be expressed via this like technique where like you said it blew my mind when they opened up this editor and they were showing like genes and copying and pasting from open source almost like i guess what we do for stack overflow no we don't definitely copy code from stack overflow they were copying various open source you know genes into these plasmids checking. It had like a linter equivalent, but then they would open up one of these and it's literally just the letters, right? It's just, and they could go in and make slight tweaks because some equivalencies are, they matter, but you can swap them. And it just like, it got me super excited until I realized just how much like time,|||比如发光蛋白质，甚至是通过这种技术表达的牛奶成分或其他东西，就像你说的，当他们打开这个编辑器并展示基因，从开源代码库复制粘贴时，我的脑洞大开——差不多就像我们在 Stack Overflow 上做的那样，不，我们绝对没有从 Stack Overflow 上复制代码——他们将各种开源的（你知道的）基因复制到这些质粒中并进行检查。它有类似 linter（代码检查工具）的东西，但随后他们打开其中一个，字面意思上它就只是字母，对吧？它就只是字母，他们可以进去做一些微小的调整，因为有些等价关系很重要，但你可以替换它们。这让我超级兴奋，直到我意识到需要花多少时间，|||because I know nothing about biology. So to go from like where I am to like making mine glow in the dark bread is a very big leap. Yeah, it's incredible. Actually, it's it's really exciting to see. In my opinion, one of the most exciting things is that this space is now open to people who are not, you know, bench scientists. So you can be a computational person and you can contribute to the bioinformatics space in ways like you were doing or by designing more efficient algorithms or even more software-oriented solutions|||因为我对生物学一无所知。所以从我现在的水平跨越到做出会让面包在黑暗中发光的程度，是一个非常巨大的飞跃。是的，这太不可思议了。实际上，看到这些真的让人很兴奋。在我看来，最令人兴奋的事情之一就是，这个领域现在向那些不是（你知道的）实验室科学人员开放了。所以你可以是一个从事计算的人才，你可以通过像你刚才所做的方式，或者通过设计更高效的算法，甚至是通过更倾向于软件的解决方案来为生物信息学领域做出贡献，|||like better parsing libraries to parse and create the text file formats that are used to share DNA sequencing information or coming up with a more efficient text-based representation of that data. So it's actually really exciting to see all the progress going on there. It is. I was excited to learn that I kind of, I guess, cynically assumed most of that software would be horrendously expensive. And it turns out now most of it you can kind of just like get, you know, it's just like open source, or the company that does a synthesis will provide it for free or whatever. So that got me kind of excited, too. So you came|||比如更好的解析库来解析和创建用于共享 DNA 测序信息的文本文件格式，或者想出一种更高效的基于文本的数据表示方法。所以看到那里的所有进展真的让人非常兴奋。确实如此。我很兴奋地了解到——我以前可能有点悲观地认为大部分此类软件会贵得离谱。结果现在大部分软件你基本上都可以直接